Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tools like Terraform manage cloud network resources by leveraging provider-specific APIs to declaratively define, provision, and maintain cloud infrastructure through configuration files (e.g., HCL) that specify desired state, while Terraform’s state management system tracks actual resource attributes and synchronizes them via the `terraform apply` workflow, ensuring idempotent deployments and rollback capabilities via `terraform destroy`.

For example, a Terraform configuration for an AWS VPC with subnets and route tables would use the `aws_vpc`, `aws_subnet`, and `aws_route_table` resources with explicit CIDR blocks, availability zones, and route associations, while the `terraform plan` command validates changes against the current state before execution:

```hcl
resource "aws_vpc" "main" {
  cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16"
  tags = { Name = "prod-vpc" }
}

resource "aws_subnet" "public" {
  vpc_id     = aws_vpc.main.id
  cidr_block = "10.0.1.0/24"
  availability_zone = "us-east-1a"
  tags = { Name = "public-subnet" }
}

resource "aws_route_table" "public" {
  vpc_id = aws_vpc.main.id
  route {
    cidr_block = "0.0.0.0/0"
    gateway_id = aws_internet_gateway.main.id
  }
  tags = { Name = "public-rt" }
}

resource "aws_route_table_association" "public" {
  subnet_id      = aws_subnet.public.id
  route_table_id = aws_route_table.public.id
}
```

