When hunting for C&C beaconing, engineers typically search for **unusual periodic traffic patterns** (e.g., DNS queries, HTTP/HTTPS requests, or ICMP pings) to known malicious domains/IPs, **small payloads with large intervals** (e.g., 10-30 bytes every 5-60 minutes), or **suspicious port usage** (e.g., non-standard ports like 443, 8080, or 53 for C2 traffic), often leveraging **statistical anomalies** (e.g., deviation from baseline traffic) or **behavioral baselines** (e.g., using tools like Zeek, Suricata, or Splunk for pattern matching).

For example, in **Zeek (Bro)**, you could use a script to detect beaconing via DNS queries with a fixed interval:
```zeek
event dns_query(d: DNS::DNSQuery) {
    if (d.qtype == DNS::TYPE_A && d.qname in { "malicious-domain[.]com", "evil[.]io" }) {
        log(dns_beacon: d);
    }
}
```
Or in **Suricata**, use a rule like:
```suricata
alert tcp any any -> any any (msg:"T1059.003 Beaconing - Suspicious DNS Query"; content:"malicious-domain"; threshold:type threshold, track by_src, count 5, seconds 300; classtype:trojan-activity; sid:1000001; rev:1;)
```

